                  <provenance type="#e"><orgName role="fmo" key="mlgbOrg_426">Oxford, Oxfordshire.
                        Balliol College (Domus de Balliolo)</orgName><p>‘At the foot of 150,
                        perhaps in the scribe’s hand, is frater Johannes Beruerii; one of this name
                        occurs as bachelor of canon law in a Paris list of A.D. 1379 in the
                        Chartularium univ. Paris., ed. H. Denifle and E. Chatelain, III (1894) 277.
                        Along the top edge of i in red, partly cut away, is: Hunc librum vendidit
                        magister Johannes Carso magistro Guillelmo Pulcrinepotis in lugo (?) pretio
                        x scutis auri; this I take to be the Guillaume Beauneveu who appears at
                        Paris as mater of arts and bachelor of divinity in 1403, and later attended
                        the Council of Constance; ibid. iv (1897)98, and 108, note 21. The volume
                        found its way to Italy, for also on i in a 15th-cent. Italian hand is:
                        (Alesandro de Ales sopra lined through) Petrus de Candia sopra le sentenzie
                        d.xij. In Italy, no doubt, Gray bought it, and later, if the offset of the
                        initial is correctly read, had his arms painted on the first leaf.’ (Mynors,
                        Balliol, 49)‘Liber domus de Balliolo in Oxon’ | ex dono Reverendi in Christo
                        patris et | domini Willelmi Gray Eliensis episcopi’ (fol.
                     iiv)</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">William Gray (c. 1414–1478), bishop of
                     Ely</provenance>
